dium", "notes": ["Open seat: Brian Kemp (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Georgia's open governor's race between Democrat Keisha Lance Bottoms and Republican Rick Jackson is one of the closest contests on the 2026 map. The state leans narrowly Republican, and Brian Kemp's departure leaves both parties fighting for a seat neither can take for granted. Most nonpartisan raters call it a Tossup, with a couple rating Bottoms the narrow favorite.\n\nRecent polls give Jackson a slim edge, though several come from Republican-leaning sponsors, and averages across outlets cluster within a point of even. Jackson holds an enormous financial advantage, having raised roughly twenty times more than Bottoms, and the airwaves reflect it. Bottoms counters with a favorable midterm climate for Democrats and her Atlanta profile. Prediction markets split between the two, pricing the race at essentially even odds, and reported ticket-splitting between the governor and Senate races adds uncertainty to any single reading.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6414, "margin": 1.75}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.5561, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.5612, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.5894, "margin": 0.8}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5413, "margin": 0.8}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.5396, "margin": 0.8}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.5736, "margin": 0.8}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.463, "margin": -0.7}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.5513, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.5602, "margin": 0.5}]}